==============================================================================================
(C) Residual span>N*(k): why pointwise domination is lossy (the omitted-index gap)
==============================================================================================
  For E=(0,1,..,k-2, BIG) the raw bound is a(BIG+1) -> 1, but true meas(S7(E)) stays
  bounded. The gap a(BIG+1)-meas(S7(E)) is the mass of theta where ONLY an omitted
  index would have supplied a missing residue. We tabulate it (EXACT):
  k=8, N*=7, cap_k=0.3815:
      BIG  span    a(span+1)    meas(S7(E))   gap(thrown away)  true<=cap?
        8     8      0.41616        0.20833            0.20782        True
       10    10      0.58146        0.19048            0.39099        True
       12    12      0.67593        0.20238            0.47355        True
       20    20      0.81072        0.19048            0.62024        True
       40    40      0.90744        0.19048            0.71696        True
       80    80      0.95420        0.19762            0.75658        True
  => pointwise domination discards exactly the un-occupied-residue mass; it is sharp
     ONLY at E=AP (full occupancy), which is precisely why N*(8)=7 = AP itself.

==============================================================================================
(D) Occupancy refinement: does a TIGHTER super-set than AP_{N+1} help?
==============================================================================================
  Subset monotonicity holds for ANY E subset F (index sets): meas(S7(E))<=meas(S7(F)).
  Test: instead of F=AP_{span+1}, use the SCALE-REDUCED hull. By scale-invariance
  meas(S7(E))=meas(S7(E/g)). Does dividing out a common structure shrink the needed F?
  E=(0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14)  -> reduced (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7)  meas=481/1470(0.3272)  scale-inv ok=True
     reduced span=7, occupancy=1=1.000, raw AP_8 bound=0.3272
  E=(0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 14)  -> reduced (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 14)  meas=683/2940(0.2323)  scale-inv ok=True
     reduced span=14, occupancy=8/15=0.533, raw AP_15 bound=0.7280
  E=(0, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21)  -> reduced (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7)  meas=481/1470(0.3272)  scale-inv ok=True
     reduced span=7, occupancy=1=1.000, raw AP_8 bound=0.3272
  CONCLUSION: subset-domination by an AP super-set is the BEST set-containment bound
  available (AP_{span+1} is the unique minimal index-interval hull); occupancy<1 is
  exactly the slack the bound cannot recover. A tighter certificate must be MEASURE-
  based (moment/Sturmian-word), not set-containment -- consistent with THM-536's
  'irreducibly aggregate' and HYP-2608's death of monotone descent.

==============================================================================================
(E) Wide-spread complement: the residual span>N*(k) handled by SMALLNESS, not domination
==============================================================================================
  For wide/dissociated E, meas(S7(E)) -> M7(k) (tiny). The danger flagged in HYP-2608
  is the RESONANT family where some offset w == 0 mod 7 (apex-prime-7). We sweep wide
  primitive E and report max meas(S7); compare to cap_k. (EXACT.)
  k=8, cap_k=0.3815:
    stranger-pull-in {0..6}+{w}, w<60: max meas(S7)=0.32721 at (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7) (<=cap)
      among RESONANT w==0 mod7: max meas(S7)=0.32721 at (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7) (<=cap)
    random wide (span<45, 4000 draws): max meas(S7)=0.10986 at (0, 3, 6, 12, 18, 19, 30, 36) (<=cap)
  k=9, cap_k=0.4943:
    stranger-pull-in {0..7}+{w}, w<60: max meas(S7)=0.41616 at (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8) (<=cap)
      among RESONANT w==0 mod7: max meas(S7)=0.37211 at (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 21) (<=cap)
    random wide (span<45, 4000 draws): max meas(S7)=0.22783 at (0, 2, 4, 12, 14, 20, 21, 22, 24) (<=cap)

==============================================================================================
(F) HONEST RESIDUAL ACCOUNTING: what is covered, what is not
==============================================================================================
  Decompose all primitive E (|E|=k) by span s=max(E):
    (i)  s <= N*(k)              -> CERTIFIED by subset domination (PROVED set-containment).
    (ii) N*(k) < s <= B_box(k)   -> covered by the EXHAUSTIVE bounded-span checks
                                    (HYP-2603 boxes: 0 violations). FINITE, mod scale.
    (iii) s > B_box(k)           -> the wide-spread regime; meas(S7) small (sweep (E)),
                                    but NOT yet a closed-form proof. = the open piece.
  k= 8: subset-domination certifies span<=7; exhaustive box checked to span<=18; wide-spread span>18 = residual (smallness, unproved closed-form).
  k= 9: subset-domination certifies span<=8; exhaustive box checked to span<=17; wide-spread span>17 = residual (smallness, unproved closed-form).
  k=10: subset-domination certifies span<=10; exhaustive box checked to span<=16; wide-spread span>16 = residual (smallness, unproved closed-form).
  k=11: subset-domination certifies span<=13; exhaustive box checked to span<=16; wide-spread span>16 = residual (smallness, unproved closed-form).
  k=12: subset-domination certifies span<=21; exhaustive box checked to span<=16; wide-spread span>16 = residual (smallness, unproved closed-form).
  k=13: subset-domination certifies span<=21; exhaustive box checked to span<=16; wide-spread span>16 = residual (smallness, unproved closed-form).

  KEY QUANTITATIVE TAKEAWAY for the writeup:
    k=8: of 31788 primitive shapes (span<=18), subset-domination alone certifies 1 (0%);
          the remaining 31787 are certified ONLY by direct computation (exhaustive), with 0 cap-violations.

  RIGOROUS STATUS OF THIS ANGLE:
   * meas(S7(AP_m)) = Sturmian partial-sum cover-time, PROVED equal to breakpoint engine,
     PROVED non-decreasing and =0 for m<=6 (exact, m<=22).
   * subset domination => meas(S7(E)) <= a(span+1): PROVED set-containment (THM-536),
     here QUANTIFIED to certify exactly span<=N*(k) (N*=7,8,10,13,~ for k=8..11).
   * The route ALONE is structurally incapable of closing wide span (occupancy<1 slack);
     it must be PAIRED with (ii) the finite bounded-span check and (iii) a genuine
     wide-spread smallness bound. (iii) remains the open piece (HYP-2608 route (a)).
   * NET: subset-domination is a clean, PROVED, but PARTIAL certificate (the small-span
     stratum). It does NOT close LRC(14) by itself; it reduces the open set to wide span.

DONE.
